\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's real slips; stocks fall on Petrobras row  \b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2006\par 17:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened Thursday, pressured by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while stocks edged lower as heavyweight Petrobras stumbled on a row with Bolivia's government.  \par \par \uc2 Fears about potentially softer global commodities prices and slower global economic growth also weighed on local asset prices, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The real closed 0.28 percent weaker at 2.164 per U.S. dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again bought dollars to build foreign reserves and prevent the real, which is near a 5-year high, from strengthening too much.  \par \par \uc2 The Bovespa index of the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange fell 1.08 percent to 36,153 points.  \par \par \uc2 Data showing retail sales in the United States grew more than expected in August, but at a slower pace than the previous month, along with a rise in import prices renewed concerns about inflation risks and deceleration of economic growth, just a week before a Federal Reserve monetary policy meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Markets are waiting for U.S. consumer price data on Friday which could give more signs about the Fed's likely decision on interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 At the stock market, state-run oil giant Petrobras fell 2.9 percent to 39.53 reais after Bolivia moved to enforce a nationalization of the Brazilian company's refineries in the Andean country.  \par \par \uc2 Telemar shares rose 4.92 percent to 64 reais, following a 6.64 percent rise on Wednesday, with investors betting that a share restructuring plan could be approved.  \par \par \uc2 Brazilians vote in a presidential election on Oct. 1 but in contrast with four years ago, markets have been calm. President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva is expected to win reelection and has promised to continue with his conservative fiscal policies.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060914e29e001ks\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
