\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil's Real Closes Slightly Stronger In Volatile Trade  \b0\par\par\uc2 477 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 2006\par 17:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2006, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Jun 23, 2006 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real closed slightly higher Friday after a day of volatile trade as export dollar inflows counterbalanced earlier concerns that Federal Reserve could extend their tightening monetary policy at a meeting next week.  \par \par \uc2 The real ended at BRL2.230 to the dollar in spot contract trading on the Brazilian Mercantile and Futures Exchange, or BMF, up from Thursday's close of BRL2.233.  \par \par \uc2 According to analysts, the real was pressured as investors awaited further direction on U.S. interest rates. The Federal Reserve's policy-setting committee, which next meets June 28-29, is expected to raise the target federal-funds rate by 25 basis points to 5.25%.  \par \par \uc2 Rising interest rates in the U.S. and other developed nations typically draw investments away from emerging markets such as Brazil.  \par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  drifted in the morning on concerns that the U.S. monetary tightening cycle would be extended before rallying in mid afternoon due to an influx of export dollars, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Worries about U.S. rate hikes had caused the Turkish lira to slide sharply in the morning before \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fundamentals are much stronger than those of many other emerging markets and the real weathered the storm well, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Local markets largely shrugged off a mixed report on U.S. durable good demand.  \par \par \uc2 May durable-goods orders fell by 0.3% to a seasonally adjusted $208.67 billion, slightly less than the 0.5% decline expected, as orders for commercial aircraft declined. However, orders for nondefense capital goods excluding aircraft rose 1%, reversing a 1.9% decline the previous month.  \par \par \uc2 The health of nondefense orders indicates underlying U.S. economic strength.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, benign Brazilian inflation data will give the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more incentive to extend the loosening cycle on monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian Census Bureau announced that Brazil's benchmark IPCA inflation index fell 0.15% in the month-long period through mid-June compared with a 0.27% rise in the same period to mid-May.  \par \par \uc2 The decline was greater than the 0.07% forecast by market platers.  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lowered the benchmark Selic interest rate eight times in a row since September. The rate currently stands at 15.25% per year.  \par \par \uc2 Local interest rate contracts fell Friday amid the quiet inflation outlook.  \par \par \uc2 The day's most actively traded contract, the January 2007 contract, fell to 14.80% from 14.81% on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The contract's project probable annualized interest charges for the overnight interbank rate at future dates.  \par \par \uc2 The actual overnight interbank rate for Frday was 15.15%, stable on the day.  \par \par \uc2 -By Alastair Stewart; Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145 1479; alastair.stewart@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 06-23-06 1526ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020060623e26n0020b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
