\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Brazil spends $11.7 bln on foreign debt buybacks  \b0\par\par\uc2 242 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juni 2006\par 16:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details)  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil, June 2 (Reuters) - Brazil's Treasury on Friday said it had spent $11.7 billion since Jan. 1 buying back foreign debt as part of a broad program started in February to cut its external debt load by up to $20 billion.  \par \par \uc2 About $6.6 billion was used to buy back Brady bonds.  \par \par \uc2 It said it had bought back a third of Global bonds maturing between 2007 and 2009, and a quarter of the bonds due in 2010.  \par \par \uc2 For bonds denominated in euros, about 10 percent of debt due through 2007 was repurchased, along with 16.5 percent of debt due in 2010.  \par \par \uc2 The Treasury said it has focused on buying shorter-term debt to lengthen the average maturity of its foreign bonds and reduce refinancing risks.  \par \par \uc2 The buybacks, which are more affordable for the government as the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, trades near 5-year highs, also may help Brazil eventually move closer to investment grade status.  \par \par \uc2 "There is an improvement in the principal indicators used by agencies specialized in risk evaluation to evaluate Brazilian sovereign debt," the Treasury said.  \par \par \uc2 International reserves have been used to pay for the buybacks. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in recent months, buying dollars to build up reserves.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-BRAZIL-DEBT (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060605e26200152\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pic