\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Opens Weaker On Palocci,  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Ideas  \b0\par\par\uc2 497 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 09:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2006, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Mar 21, 2006 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real opened weaker against the U.S. dollar Tuesday as investors prepared for the possibility that Finance Minister Antonio Palocci may be forced to resign over allegations of corruption, and the possibility that the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will impose a tax on short-term foreign capital inflows.  \par \par \uc2 The real opened at BRL2.158 to the dollar in spot contract trading on the Brazilian Mercantile and Futures Exchange, weaker from Monday's close of BRL2.149 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Last week, a new witness linked Palocci, a market favorite, to an alleged kickback scheme run by the ruling Workers' Party, or PT.  \par \par \uc2 Palocci has repeatedly denied involvement in any corruption, but political opposition leaders are ramping up pressure to force the finance minister out.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said that economic policy likely wouldn't change significantly if Palocci departs but he is a key figure in the government and his resignation would weaken President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also talked of rumors that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may introduce restrictions on foreign short-term capital inflows in order to weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The bank is considering raising taxes on short term inflows that aren't destined for stocks or Treasury note issues, according to the local business daily Valor Economico.  \par \par \uc2 Until recently, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been using \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt swaps to contain the rise of the real. Under the scheme, the institution took dollar-denominated debt and offered paper linked to local interest rates in return. This allowed foreign investors to take advantage of Brazil's towering local interest rates without bringing dollars into Brazil. However, the institution ceased these \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  two weeks days ago as, according to analysts, the bank had canceled out all its dollar-denominated debt and was uncomfortable about taking an active futures position in the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said there was little new in the comments of Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke after the market close Monday. His speech to the Economic Club of New York indicated U.S. fed funds would continue to rise but this would not greatly affect the liquidity in emerging markets, said one analyst.  \par \par \uc2 The market will be waiting for the release of February current account data later in the morning. Investors are divided over what figure will be announced, with estimates ranging from a deficit of $1 million to a surplus of $700 million according to a survey of analysts conducted by the local Estado news agency.  \par \par \uc2 Any figure at the lower end of this range would lead the real to weaken, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Investors are also waiting for the release of the U.S. producer price index later in the day. The index is expected to have declined in February due to lower energy costs.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Alastair Stewart, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3145-1479; brazil@dowjones.com\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 03-21-06 0828ET  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020060321e23l004qm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
