\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil candidate Alckmin wants new economic policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 428 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 16:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Brazil, March 16 (Reuters) - Brazilian presidential hopeful Geraldo Alckmin said on Thursday that if elected in October he would change economic policies that have been favored by Wall Street but which he blames for causing sluggish growth.  \par \par \uc2 "The economic model is wrong," said Alckmin, who is supported by many Brazilian industrialists and other corporate chieftains, said in a broadcast by CBN radio.  \par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy is too conservative, there is no reason to have real interest rates of 12 percent," said the governor of Brazil's most populous state of Sao Paulo and the leading opposition contender for the presidency.  \par \par \uc2 He was chosen Tuesday as the candidate of the centrist Brazilian Social Democracy Party and trails President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva in opinion polls by wide margins.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's real interest rate, or the nominal rate of 16.5 percent less inflation of about 4.5 percent, is about 12 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Banks have posted record profits during the administration of Lula, a former union leader who adopted economic policies favored by investors after winning in a 2002 landslide.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says high interest rates are needed to control inflation, but many manufacturing companies disagree.  \par \par \uc2 Corporate chiefs supporting Alckmin complain that economic growth under Lula has averaged less than 3 percent a year.  \par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate is overvalued," he said of Brazil's real <BRBY>, which is near a five-year high against the dollar. Exporters fear their sales will retreat this year, hurting the agricultural, clothing and shoe sectors.  \par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't disregard a dirty float," he said. "You can do \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has heavily bought dollars on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since November to build up foreign reserves, but the purchases have done little to dampen the real's gains.  \par \par \uc2 FISCAL POLICY  \par \par \uc2 "Fiscal policy is bad and we need to improve the quality of spending," Alckmin said.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil has a tax burden like a Western European country, but inefficient public services, regressive tax rules, and inflexible spending requirements.  \par \par \uc2 Most of the budget is eaten up by debt payments, salaries for civil servants and deficit-ridden pension and social security funds. Money for capital investments is scant.  \par \par \uc2 "The government ... didn't control superfluous costs," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Even though he has made efforts to close budget deficits, Lula has allowed spending growth to outpace economic growth, frustrating economists who say the added outlays have contributed to inflation.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-BRAZIL-ALCKMIN|LANGEN|ABN|E|U|RBN|M|G|RNP|DNP|PGE  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060316e23g0022q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
