\par\par \b \uc2 = Brazil's Foreign Tax Breaks Add Fuel To Real's March  \b0\par\par\uc2 933 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Februar 2006\par 21:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2006, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Feb 16, 2006 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 \f2 By Alastair Stewart\par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian government's decision to offer tax breaks to foreign investors in government bonds added further fuel to the ramping local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 While Brazilian officials gave investors a signal two weeks ago that the rules will change, the psychological impact of the formal announcement acted as a trigger for more overseas money to flow in.  \par \par \uc2 "The market knew this was coming, but investors are looking for motives to pour money into Brazil and this announcement is seen as another green light," said Andre Deluca of Sao Paulo-based brokerage Intra.  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real opened 0.8% higher at 2.12 to the dollar on Thursday, its strongest level since March 2001.  \par \par \uc2 A presidential decree published Thursday exempts overseas investors from a 15% capital gains tax and a 0.38% financial transaction tax on buying government bonds. The step is an attempt to switch Brazil's public debt profile to a more home-based one.  \par \par \uc2 The long-term impact of the move on the exchange rate won't not be great, but it definitely contributes to the positive feeling investors have about Brazil, said Adauto Lima, an economist at investment bank WestLB in Sao Paulo.  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real has strengthened 22% against the dollar over the past 12 months, fueled by heavy export inflows and foreign investment in Brazilian stocks. Now, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatens to test the two-to-one barrier in the next few weeks, traders and analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "The pressure of export dollars on the futures market is extreme," said Mauro Giorgi at local brokerage Geracao Futuro.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil continues to register record trade surpluses despite the rise of the real against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Exporters have been warning of an imminent drop in shipments for the last two years, but that just hasn't happened. Between Jan. 1 and Feb. 12, 2006, Brazil's trade surplus totaled $3.87 billion, up 19% on year.  \par \par \uc2 In part, this is because the cost of financing Brazilian exports has dropped sharply over the past year along with an improvement in Brazil's credit risk rating, offsetting the impact of the strengthening real, said Geracao Futuro's Giorgi.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a large portion of Brazil's exports are denominated not in dollars, but in euros and yen, which has allowed the country's shipments to retain their price edge, according to Ricardo Amorim, head of Latin America research at WestLB.  \par \par \uc2 In addition, other major commodity exporters, like Brazil, have also seen their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise.  \par \par \uc2 But perhaps most importantly, astronomical gains in global prices of commodities - a key part of Brazil's trade balance - have more than offset any losses incurred because of the stronger real.  \par \par \uc2 The seemingly insatiable appetite of foreign investors for emerging market assets amid abundant global liquidity is also driving up the exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 "The outlook for Brazilian stocks looks extremely positive. It seems foreign investors have their eyes firmly fixed on the stocks here and if the international situation continues to be benign, it will stay that way," said Intra's Deluca.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the improvement in Brazil's risk rating only serves to reinforce the 'virtuous cycle' of investments and brings the prospect of an upgrade to the speculative grade double-B-minus rating on Brazil's sovereign debt ever closer.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  spot and futures market since October - moves that most analysts view as an attempt to limit the rise of the real.  \par \par \uc2 However, since January, the bank has chosen to pare back the level of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps it offers after it canceled out virtually all its dollar-denominated debt. The swaps are used to shift the government's liability away from dollar-denominated debt to local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to construct "a wall in the middle of the sea to contain a tidal wave," said WestLB's Amorim. "It's an impossible task."  \par \par \uc2 In the last quarter of 2005, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying up to $500 million a day on the spot market, but it has now reduced those purchases to between around $80 million to $140 million in an apparent admission that it can't contain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Given these factors, most analysts now believe the real will inevitably hit the 2.00 level against the dollar. But few are willing to believe it will fall below that level.  \par \par \uc2 If the dollar hits two, the outcry from Brazil's industry will be enormous and the government may be forced to take "administrative" steps to reverse the situation, according to some analysts.  \par \par \uc2 Those steps could include an end to the rule requiring exporters to repatriate dollars. Currently, exporters must bring dollars into the country within 210 days of completing a transaction and can't use these funds to pay offshore costs or for imports.  \par \par \uc2 "If we hit two-to-one, the market believes the government will be forced to do something more dynamic," said Geracao Futuro's Giorgi.  \par \par \uc2 But a sharp reduction in interest rates, which analysts said would be the most effective measure, is unlikely to happen as this government appears firmly focused on controlling inflation, analysts and traders added.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been easing its monetary policy since September but the Selic base interest rate still stands at a towering 17.25%.  \par \par \uc2 -By Alastair Stewart, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145 1479; alastair Stewart@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 02-16-06 1205ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020060216e22g0072o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
