\par\par \b \uc2 If  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  stops,  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  real may gain 10 pct  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Terry Wade  \par \uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2006\par 19:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jan 31 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real, which outperformed most of the world's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2005, could rally as much as 10 percent in the first quarter of this year if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stops entering the market, economists say.  \par \par \uc2 Demand for the real <BRBY> and dollar inflows are heavy as Brazilian companies repatriate record exports and proceeds from overseas bond sales. At the same time, a likely end to U.S. interest rate hikes has made foreign investors even hungrier for high returns in Latin America's largest country, where the benchmark rate is 17.25 percent.  \par \par \uc2 What is more, most economists think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has acted to weaken the real twice a day since November, will change tactics sometime in March. It could do that by no longer purchasing dollars, stopping the sale of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, or both.  \par \par \uc2 If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real's rally might be very swift. In January the real managed to gain about 4 percent even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly waded into the market.  \par \par \uc2 Ricardo Amorim, head of Latin American Research at WestLB, said he expects the real to strengthen to 2 per U.S. dollar in the next several months.  \par \par \uc2 Others also see a rally from its current level of 2.21.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not unreasonable that the real would go to 2 by the end of the first quarter," said Douglas Smith, chief economist for the Americas at Standard Chartered Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "2.20 is our official line but it could go lower for sure. Some of our traders think it will easily go to 2.1."  \par \par \uc2 Since November, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has heavily sold \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps that allow investors to get exposure to Brazil's high interest rates, currently at 17.25 percent, without buying reais.  \par \par \uc2 The swaps also convert dollar-denominated debt into real-based debt.  \par \par \uc2 To accompany its swap sales, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought dollars heavily on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since October to boost international reserves, now at about $53.8 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The combined efforts have cut Brazil's net foreign debt to around $10 billion when reserves are discounted.  \par \par \uc2 At its current pace of entering the market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have canceled the exchange-rate risk of its net foreign debt within two months.  \par \par \uc2 "By selling swaps they are actually increasing floating-rate debt, which is contrary to their stated goal. So I think they are going to stop," said John Welch, senior vice president for sovereign strategy at Lehman Brothers in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "It would also make sense for them to stop buying dollars" because of negative carry.  \par \par \uc2 When the bank holds dollars, it must invest them abroad at low interest rates -- of, say, 5 percent -- whereas when it invests reais locally it can earn at double-digit rates.  \par \par \uc2 Some economists say making money is not one of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's priorities and that dollar buying could continue.  \par \par \uc2 "Central banks aren't big on making profits on their reserves. And nearly all have the problem of negative carry when buying dollars," said Smith of Standard Chartered. "They key thing for them is liquidity instead of returns."  \par \par \uc2 RATE CUTS MAY STEM RALLY  \par \par \uc2 Brazilian companies have complained the strong real is hurting the competitiveness of exports, even though the trade surplus was a record $44.76 billion in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 But a stronger real would cut inflationary pressures and allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has lowered interest rates five times since September, to loosen monetary policy more aggressively.  \par \par \uc2 Lower interest rates would also help the government further cut the burden of servicing its debt since about half of it floats on short-term interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does cut rates more aggressively, as is widely expected, it could also help stem the real's gains.  \par \par \uc2 "There's a way they can keep it from going to 2, which is to cut rates and increase the money supply," Welch said.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-BRAZIL-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 |LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060131e21v001tw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
