\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Brazil cuts all domestic dlr-linked debt exposure  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Isabel Versiani and Terry Wade  \par \uc2 690 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2006\par 15:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comment from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president)  \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA, Brazil, Jan 23 (Reuters) - Brazil's government eliminated all of its exposure to domestic dollar-linked debt in January, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday, as officials work to cut the riskiness of the public debt load.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said it may continue selling interest rate-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps that have been used to cancel out dollar-linked liabilities, which were originally sold to prevent the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening.  \par \par \uc2 On Monday the bank sold $201.5 million in swaps, suggesting it will start using the swaps to cancel out exposure to foreign debt denominated in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Credit rating agencies have frowned on the use of dollar-linked liabilities, which in 2002 comprised as much as 40 percent of domestic debt.  \par \par \uc2 The swaps have also been sold to pull back the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real <BRBY>, from some of its highest levels in 4-1/2 years.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, which have been sold 41 times since November, allow investors to get exposure to Brazil's high interest rates, currently at 17.25 percent, without having to buy reais.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has bought dollars 74 times since October to blunt the real and boost international reserves, which have tripled in the past few years to $53.8 billion.  \par \par \uc2 But some economists say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is running out of room to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has about $62 billion in external sovereign debt, or only about $8 billion in net foreign debt when reserves are discounted.  \par \par \uc2 At the current pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have canceled the exchange-rate risk of its net foreign debt within two months.  \par \par \uc2 At that point, some economists say, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have a more difficult time justifying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When the bank holds dollars, it must invest them abroad at low interest rates -- of, say, 5 percent -- whereas when it invests reais locally it can earn at double-digit rates.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  is getting smaller and smaller. And there will be a deep structural adjustment that happens very fast when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stops \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ricardo Amorim, chief economist at WestLB in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "If international liquidity keeps up, we will see the real stronger than 2 per dollar in the first part of the year."  \par \par \uc2 That means the real could rally 13 percent from its current level of about 2.26.  \par \par \uc2 Asked if there are any plans to stop selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps or purchasing dollars on the spot market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles told Reuters: "Unfortunately we don't comment about this subject."  \par \par \uc2 TOTAL DEBT LOAD GROWS  \par \par \uc2 Brazilian companies and Trade Minister Luiz Furlan have complained the strong real is hurting the competitiveness of exports.  \par \par \uc2 But economists say Brazil, which had a record $44.76 billion trade surplus in 2005, could afford to see its trade surplus shrink.  \par \par \uc2 A stronger real would cut inflationary pressures and allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has cut interest rates five times since September, to loosen monetary policy more aggressively. Lower interest rates would also help the government further cut the burden of servicing its debt.  \par \par \uc2 Even as dollar-linked debt fell, Brazil's federal domestic debt at the end of 2005 was larger than ever.  \par \par \uc2 It also remained risky because the average length of debt is short, at about 27 months, and half of the debt floats on short-term interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The total domestic debt at the end of December was 979.7 billion reais ($433.5 billion), up from 810.3 billion reais debt at the end of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 The share of debt linked to the overnight Selic interest rate rose marginally to 53.3 percent in December from 52.41 percent a year before.  \par \par \uc2 The share of fixed-rate domestic debt rose to 27.86 percent from 20.09 a year before. Much of the rest of the domestic debt load is tied to inflation indexes.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-BRAZIL-DEBT (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|ABN|D|M|E|RBN|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060123e21n001h9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
