\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil stocks hit record on rate cut;  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  off  \b0\par\par\uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2006\par 15:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil, Jan 19 (Reuters) - Brazil's stock market rallied nearly 3 percent on Thursday to a record high after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates to spur economic growth, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped for the third straight day.  \par \par \uc2 The Bovespa index <.BVSP> of the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange finished up 2.94 percent to 36,858 after peaking earlier in the day within 21 points of the 37,000 mark.  \par \par \uc2 All shares climbed, with blue chips like state-run oil company Petrobras and mining giant Companhia Vale do Rio Doce, or CVRD, posting healthy gains. Petrobras shares rose 3.1 percent to 43.7 reais, while CVRD rose nearly 3 percent to 94.9 reais.  \par \par \uc2 The market rallied as investors bet falling interest rates would help revive the Brazilian economy, which is struggling to bounce back from a slump in the third quarter of 2005.  \par \par \uc2 With inflation under control, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered its benchmark lending rate late Wednesday by 0.75 percentage points to 17.25 percent a year. The reduction was the biggest rate cut since the bank started easing monetary policy last September.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real <BRBY>, lost ground on a lower benchmark interest rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It closed off 0.22 percent at 2.329 per U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Lower domestic rates tend to put pressure on the real, which is trading near its strongest level in 4-1/2 years and worrying exporters.  \par \par \uc2 "Even though rates are high, the bias is downward and the market expects the government will cut more in the next few months," said Fabio Pfaender, market analyst at the Liquidez brokerage in Sao Paulo.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sought to blunt the real's gains by regularly buying dollars on the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps linked to short-term interest rates that cut demand for reais.  \par \par \uc2 The bank sold $401.4 million in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps on Thursday for the 39th time since November. The swaps also cancel out old dollar-linked debt that was originally sold to stop the real from weakening.  \par \par \uc2 The bank also bought dollars on the spot market for the 72nd time since early October.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have helped bolster \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves, which finished 2005 at $53.8 billion, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday. In December alone the bank bought $4 billion on the spot market, bringing its total purchases in 2005 to $21.49 billion.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060119e21j001bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
