\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  To Ease As Trade Surplus Ebbs  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Alastair Stewart  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 975 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2006\par 20:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may deny it, but to the rest of the market it clearly appears that the aim of the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is to counter the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The bank's heavy hand will only withdraw when net trade dollar inflows recede, analysts reckon.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is trying to kid everyone, but nobody is buying it. They are waiting for the first sign that exports will slip and, more significantly, that imports will grow," said Thiago Davino, economist at local GRC Visao brokerage.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary guardians began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily after the real strengthened by as much as 25% in the first eleven months of 2005. By buying dollars on the spot market and then offering so-called reverse swaps, which exchange dollar exposure for local interest rate returns, the bank has halted the real's march.  \par \par \uc2 As of Thursday, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at around BRL2.32 to the dollar after reaching a four-year high of BRL2.16 in November. The real's appreciation during the calendar year of 2005 was held down to 14.2%.  \par \par \uc2 "Without this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real could have hit 2-to-1," said Pedro Silveira, an economist at Sao Paulo-based GlobalInvest Asset Management.  \par \par \uc2 So when will the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  recede?  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officially said it was offering the swap contacts to offset government dollar-indexed debt in the market. But after buying $16.7 billion in dollar-linked debt since mid-November, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has now wiped out its exposure to this type of risk, according to investment bank estimates.  \par \par \uc2 The sale of swap contracts has continued, however, and is as popular with foreign investors as ever, offering them the opportunity to enjoy Brazil's towering interest rates without assuming \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. And by stemming the flow of dollars into Brazil for the length of the contracts, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is easing pressure on the exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesman said the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  would maintain its current \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, though he didn't say for how long.  \par \par \uc2 Global Invest's Silveira said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems "fully committed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at present."  \par \par \uc2 But rather than trying to control the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or set targets, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's objective appears to be to contain overshooting, as it has done in the past, said Gustavo Loyola, ex-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president, in a recent report. Loyola is a partner at the Tendencias Consulting Group in Sao Paulo.  \par \par \uc2 Back in 2002, it offered dollar-linked debt to stem an outflow of dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Two factors caused the steep appreciation of the Brazilian real last year: the extraordinary growth in the trade surplus and the arrival of speculative capital attracted by Brazil's towering interest rates. At 17.25%, Brazil's base Selic rate is one of the highest of any emerging market.  \par \par \uc2 One of these factors will have to change for the strength of the Brazilian real to ease, but it is unlikely that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lower interest rates sufficiently to cause investors to search for better returns elsewhere, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 It has been incrementally reducing the base rate since September, but the rate remains high and analysts say the monetary loosening policy will falter later this year as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  eyes inflation nervously.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  only has one objective, and that's to control inflation. It does it obsessively," said Silveira.  \par \par \uc2 The base rate has not dipped below 15% in the last 15 years and a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey of analysts, released Monday, put the rate at a stubbornly high 15% by the end of 2006.  \par \par \uc2 That leaves the trade balance. Luckily for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , clear signs are emerging that export orders are declining for the first half of the year while imports are expected to rise as local companies lose competitiveness because of the strong real.  \par \par \uc2 "The trade balance could dip as soon as February, which will allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce the amount of dollar debt swaps offered," said Davino.  \par \par \uc2 The trade surplus is expected to slip to $37 billion to $38 billion in 2006 from a record $44.76 billion in 2005, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most recent survey of economists. As a result, the current account surplus is seen falling to $8 billion in 2006 from $14.2 billion in 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The level of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been elevated over the past two months.  \par \par \uc2 On top of offering between $400 million and $600 million in swaps per day, the monetary body bought up as much as $800 million per day on the spot market during the height of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in December, according to traders. Currently it is buying around $500 million in spot purchases per day.  \par \par \uc2 The spot dollar purchases are likely to continue as it takes advantage of the strong real to build foreign reserves, thus reducing the country's exposure to the whims of international markets. Currently, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  foreign reserves stand at $56.9 billion.  \par \par \uc2 In the near term, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to pull in opposing directions by, on the one hand, buying dollars but, on the other hand, spurring dollar inflows by maintaining interest rates at elevated levels.  \par \par \uc2 "In truth, Meirelles is just easing the pressure a little, the only real way to weaken the real is to lower interest rates," said Davino.  \par \par \uc2 But reducing rates prematurely could undermine the bank's so far successful efforts against inflation, which moderated to 5.7% last year from 7.6% in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   -By Alastair Stewart, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511 3145 1479; alastair.stewart@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 19-01-06 1929GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060119e21j00105\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
