\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Closes Weaker On  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 2006\par 19:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real closed weaker against the U.S. dollar Thursday as two \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighed down the real in light trading.  \par \par \uc2 The real closed at BRL2.328 per dollar, weaker from Wednesday's close of BRL2.323 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 According to traders, weak volume and some profit-taking gave more strength to the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's double-dip in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 The bank held another in its continuing series of "reverse-swap" auctions, which allow investors to exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  linked debt for paper indexed to local interest rates. The auctions help remove an excess of dollar-linked paper from the futures market.  \par \par \uc2 The bank said it sold 8,500 of the 8,800 contracts on offer for $401.41 million. The swaps offered four maturities between October 2006 and July 2008.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also purchased dollars from the market in a snap auction late Thursday afternoon at BRL2.3345 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real was trading at BRL2.335 per dollar before the bank called the auction at 1735 GMT. The real traded stable at BRL2.335 per dollar shortly after results of the auction were announced.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began purchasing dollars at near-daily snap auctions in October as a way to build foreign reserves. It then restarted the "reverse-swap" auctions in November, after previously halting the practice in March.  \par \par \uc2 The real was also undermined by an inflation report that showed price pressures picking up.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Thursday, the private Getulio Vargas Foundation, or FGV, said Brazil's General Price index, known as the IGP-M, rose 0.82% in the 10 days through Jan. 20, compared with a fall of 0.06% for the same period of December, FGV said. The results were in line with market expectations, which had forecast a figure ranging from inflation of 0.6% to 1.1%.  \par \par \uc2 The report followed on fresh language in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate-setting statement released late Wednesday. While the bank reduced the Selic base interest rate by 75 basis points to 17.25% per year, it said that it was still on the lookout for signs of inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 In the statement, the bank said it will "attentively follow the evolution of the inflation outlook until its next meeting to then define the next steps in the monetary policy strategy that has been implemented since September 2005."  \par \par \uc2 Inflation as measured by the official IPCA index closed 2005 at 5.69%, the lowest rate since 1998's 1.65%. While the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2005 calendar-year target for IPCA inflation was 5.1%, a tolerance band allowed for a maximum inflation rate of 7%. It was the second consecutive year that inflation came in within the government's established tolerance band.  \par \par \uc2 Central bankers next meet March 7-8.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Brazil's risk spread as measured by the J.P. Morgan Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus tightened slightly during the day to a spread of 288 basis points over U.S. Treasurys as of 1844 GMT. Brazil's benchmark 2040 bond was up 0.650 at 130.750.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; 