\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Closes Weaker On US Data,  \highlight22\uc2 Interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 475 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2006\par 20:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO (Dow Jones)--The Brazilian real closed weaker against the U.S. dollar Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and disappointing economic data in the U.S. raised expectations of continued interest rate hikes by the Federal Reserve.  \par \par \uc2 The real closed at BRL2.276 per dollar, weaker from Thursday's close of BRL2.265 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 According to traders, Friday saw some profit-taking and caution by investors as data from the U.S. raised expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve would continue to raise interest rates in an effort to ward off inflation pressures. Rising interest rates in the U.S. tend to attract investment away from emerging markets such as Brazil.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, the U.S. Labor Department reported that its Producer Price Index, which measures price pressures before they reach the consumer, rose 0.9% in December. The increase was the most since a 1.7% jump in September. Higher gasoline prices were behind the advance.  \par \par \uc2 For the year, wholesale prices climbed 5.4%, the most since a 5.7% rise in 1990.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were also affected by news of Banco do Brasil's (BBAS3.BR) pricing of a $500 million overseas perpetual bond issue, analysts said. State-owned Banco do Brasil, the country's largest bank, priced the issue at par to yield 7.95%. Banco do Brasil raised the issue from its original forecast of $300 million after demand reached $4.5 billion, according to a person familiar with the deal.  \par \par \uc2 Brazilian debt issues have seen heated demand from overseas investors hungry for high-yield paper.  \par \par \uc2 Furthermore, the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued its purchase activities in the spot market Friday, with reverse-swap and snap auctions.  \par \par \uc2 The bank said it sold $406.5 million of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  linked debt swaps in a reverse-swap auction, selling 8,600 of the 8,800 contracts on offer. The swaps offered four maturities between October 2006 and July 2008.  \par \par \uc2 The auctions allow investors to swap dollar-linked bonds for paper indexed to local interest rates. The auctions help remove an excess of dollar-linked paper from the futures market.  \par \par \uc2 In the first 13 days of 2005, the bank has sold $4.05 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps.  \par \par \uc2 In addition, the bank bought dollars at BRL2.268 per dollar in a snap auction called at 1736 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Brazil's risk spread as measured by the J.P. Morgan Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus widened slightly during the day to a spread of 287 basis points over U.S. Treasurys as of 1855 GMT. Brazil's benchmark 2040 bond was up 0.750 at 130.600.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; (55 11) 3145-1481; jeff.fick@dowjones.com [ 13-01-06 1902GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1156 1843  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060113e21d000sx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
