\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil Real Closes Stronger In Light Holiday Trading  \b0\par\par\uc2 534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Dezember 2005\par 21:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Dec 23, 2005 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real closed stronger against the U.S. dollar on Friday in light trading before the Christmas holiday weekend, and despite two auctions by the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to purchase dollars from the market.  \par \par \uc2 The real closed at BRL2.318 per dollar, stronger from Thursday's close of BRL2.330 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was bolstered by comments from Finance Minister Antonio Palocci, who predicted a strong performance for the Brazilian economy in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 Palocci said falling interest rates and an improved investment environment will help the economy repeat in 2006 its performance of 2004, when the economy grew 4.9%.  \par \par \uc2 "The conditions for strong growth are there," Palocci said. "I believe the scenario for that is being created for 2006 is very close to that seen in 2003 and 2004."  \par \par \uc2 Palocci also cited improving sovereign debt risk, a growing foreign trade surplus and rising consumer incomes, as well as growing domestic credit and sales as strong indicators that the country is heading for a rebound over the next 12 months.  \par \par \uc2 The government of President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva has been lauded by its austere economic policies, which culminated in the early payment of debt obligations with the International Monetary Fund.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, the government was expected to pay the IMF remaining credits worth $13.4 billion, after a $2.04 billion payment Thursday. The $15.44 billion in payments cover debts that would have been due in 2006 and 2007. The government also said that will pay off its remaining $2.6 billion in debt to the Paris Club of international creditors in January.  \par \par \uc2 The payments have dented Brazil's foreign reserves, which stood at $67 billion before the IMF payments. Traders expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue its recent efforts to build reserves through market purchases into 2006.  \par \par \uc2 Palocci's positive forecast for \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy in 2006 helped offset two \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market carried out by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Early Friday afternoon, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it sold $277 million of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  linked debt swaps on offer at an auction, selling 5,800 of the 8,400 contracts on offer. The swaps offered maturities between September 2006 and July 2008.  \par \par \uc2 The auctions allow investors to swap dollar-linked bonds for paper indexed to local interest rates. The auctions help remove an excess of dollar-linked paper from the futures market.  \par \par \uc2 Later in the day, the bank bought dollars at a snap auction for BRL2.308 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real was trading at BRL2.312 per dollar shortly before the bank called the auction at 1729 GMT. The real traded slightly weaker at BRL2.319 per dollar shortly after the auction.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Brazil's risk spread as measured by the J.P. Morgan Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus stood at a spread of 303 basis points over U.S. Treasurys as of 1843 GMT. Brazil's benchmark 2040 bond was up 0.400 at 129.250.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; (55 11) 3145-1481; jeff.fick@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 12-23-05 1348ET  \par 