\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Brazil to pay off all $15.5 bln in IMF debt early  \b0\par\par\uc2 467 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2005\par 18:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments, details)  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil, Dec 13 (Reuters) - Brazil's Finance Ministry on Tuesday said it will pay back $15.5 billion to the International Monetary Fund by year-end, marking the end of a long history of financial turbulence in Latin America's largest country.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry said the decision, which would retire all liabilities owed to the multilateral lender ahead of schedule, is the "result of stronger external accounts and macroeconomic fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 Brazil was slated to repay $7.03 billion in principal to the IMF in 2006 and $8.43 billion in 2007, the ministry said.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil is expected to post record trade and current account surpluses this year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to build up low foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The combined factors have allowed the government to repay the debt early. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Henrique Meirelles, in a statement, called it "a historic moment for the country," which at times was the IMF's biggest borrower.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil obtained a series of IMF loans around the time that it devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1999 amid a balance of payments crisis. The country later renewed agreements with the IMF several times, but let its last one lapse earlier this year.  \par \par \uc2 According to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data, the prepayments would retire all debt Brazil has with the IMF.  \par \par \uc2 "This makes perfectly good sense from a couple of perspectives," said Michael Hood, strategist with Barclays Capital in New York. "It symbolically represents an exit from the financial turmoil of the '90s and 2002 and it also makes some cashflow sense because the carrying costs of holding reserves are quite high."  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's government earns low interest rates in the United States and Europe on its hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and cannot reap the benefits of the country's high benchmark interest rate of 18.5 percent. Additionally, Finance Minister Antonio Palocci said, the prepayments would save Brazil some $900 million in interest costs.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think this will change the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's actions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market because the actions there seem to have less to do with reserves and more about the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  valuation," Hood said.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's net reserves have tripled over the last few years to about $50 billion, thanks in part to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Credit rating agencies have said \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to build up foreign reserves to attain investment grade status.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were resumed in October to pull the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real <BRBY>, back from some of its strongest levels in 4-1/2 years and keep exports competitive.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-BRAZIL-IMF (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|ABX|BNX|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051213e1cd001cz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
