\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Opens Weaker On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Ideas  \b0\par\par\uc2 509 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2005\par 21:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Dec 12, 2005 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real opened slightly weaker against the U.S. dollar Monday amid speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would increase its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 The real opened at BRL2.268 per dollar, down from Friday's close of BRL2.253 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Recently, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been very active on the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market buying dollars on the spot market and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  linked debt through swap auctions.  \par \par \uc2 The auctions will continue Monday when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects to swap approximately $600 million dollar-linked debt for interest-linked paper. The results will be released at around 1630 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 But local banks are now speculating that the bank will resort to other measures to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid loud complaints from politicians and exporters that a real near four-and-a-half-year highs is hurting the economy.  \par \par \uc2 The central banks could allow exporters to delay the repatriation of dollars or it could restrict banks' ability to build short positions in the real, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Faced with this possibility, last week Brazilian banks were unwinding short positions, which reached a high for the year in November at $3.421 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Domestic banks are also wary that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will now take a more aggressive stance on interest rate cuts when it meets Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Market expectations are for a cut of 50 basis points, but one trader said that some banks had already priced in a 75-basis-point cut, resulting in some volatility in the market as domestic banks started buying January futures contracts and putting pressure on the spot market.  \par \par \uc2 News that Sao Paulo inflation came in lower than expected for the four-week period to Dec. 7 fueled that speculation.  \par \par \uc2 Sao Paulo's Fipe research institute said its consumer price index rose 0.18% in the four week period compared with an increase of 0.29% in November. The institute is associated with the University of Sao Paulo. The result came in below market forecasts, which pegged the rate between 0.23% and 0.36%.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Brazil's General Price Index, or IGP-M, was 0.06% higher in the first 10 days of December, a lower rate of acceleration than the 0.16% rise seen in the first 10 days of November. The result was in line with market expectations, which posted forecasts ranging from a drop of 0.03% to an increase of 0.21% for the period.  \par \par \uc2 The base Selic interest rate currently stands at 18.5% per year.  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real rose against the dollar for the last three sessions. However, this is only because of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the market trend is still for the real to weaken, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 With real interest rates at around 13% per year, Brazil continues to attract foreign short-term investment capital.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Alastair Stewart, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511-3145-1479; brazil@dowjones.com\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12-12-05 0757ET  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ0002