\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Brazil seen resuming growth in Q4-FinMin  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ana Nicolaci da Costa and Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 777 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2005\par 18:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with more Palocci quotes, adds WTO comments)  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Dec 2 (Reuters) - Brazilian Finance Minister Antonio Palocci said on Friday his nation's economic growth this year was likely to undershoot previous forecasts but would end 2005 on a positive note after a dip in the third quarter.  \par \par \uc2 Palocci said that growth this year could be below the government's forecast of 3.4 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's economy contracted 1.2 percent in the third quarter compared with the second quarter, marking its first fall in two years and leaving the government vulnerable to attack as 2006 elections loom.  \par \par \uc2 "If we take a look ahead, if we were to take the data from this quarter (it) indicates a positive outlook in spite of the drop in GDP which was detected for the third quarter," Palocci told journalists through a translator.  \par \par \uc2 When asked whether growth would be below 3.4 percent this year, he said: "probably below 3.4".  \par \par \uc2 When asked whether it would be below 3.00 percent he said: "I have to wait for new data."  \par \par \uc2 TRADE WRANGLE  \par \par \uc2 Palocci was speaking on the sidelines of a meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven industrialised nations.  \par \par \uc2 Both London and Washington hope the London meeting of the G7 will inject fresh life into faltering talks on trade liberalisation in the run-up to a Hong Kong meeting of 148 World Trade Organization countries in less than two weeks' time.  \par \par \uc2 Britain's Gordon Brown, who chairs the G7 this year, put much of the blame on other European nations and called for an end to subsidies for farmers in rich countries, which the OECD says total about $280 billion a year.  \par \par \uc2 Palocci said there was no guarantee that the Hong Kong talks would be an outright success but that he was optimistic.  \par \par \uc2 "Our focus tomorrow is to make an appeal to the countries that are present at the G7 and the guests, so that we can take one step forward on the question of Hong Kong," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "The proposition of the Americans and English is that we take a step forward. What I told them is that Brazil has the disposition to advance positons, but this won't be done if there isn't a more daring position on the part of the EU."  \par \par \uc2 CRIMP IN GROWTH  \par \par \uc2 Brazilian growth was hurt by high interest rates aimed at curbing inflation and a steep drop in the agricultural sector.  \par \par \uc2 Inflation has steadily declined from 12.5 percent in 2002. Next year's target is 4.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Despite the drop in third quarter growth, Palocci said there is no need for a change in Brazil's monetary policy.  \par \par \uc2 "The intensity of the policy is a legitimate debate... but I would not say that the reduction of the GDP in one quarter would be an indicator that the policy should change," Palocci said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began to cut its base Selic lending rate in September for the first time in nearly a year-and-a-half after it reached a high of 19.75 percent. The Selic rate remains one of the highest in the world after discounting inflation.  \par \par \uc2 On Nov. 23 the Selic rate was cut by half of a percentage point to 18.50 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Palocci said the appreciation of the real, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , should not be taken as a negative factor for the economy.  \par \par \uc2 He said the effect of exchange rate developments would not be the same for everyone, "but overall there has been a positive outlook."  \par \par \uc2 He said the main pressure seen on the exchange rate was from trade and foreign investments.  \par \par \uc2 "So if you take the sum of these two with all the money coming into the country, there is an appreciation of the real," Palocci added.  \par \par \uc2 "So I think the appreciation of the real cannot be seen as a negative effect, which is an effect of the trade openness...but it should be interpreted in the fact that the country's income is also increasing."  \par \par \uc2 While Palocci was meeting in London, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for the 40th time since October, offering to buy dollars to build up foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 The real held its gains and last traded 0.72 percent stronger at 2.205 per dollar <BRBY>.  \par \par \uc2 Exporters favour the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it could help pull the real back from 4-1/2 year highs that threaten to hurt sales.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-BRAZIL-PALOCCI (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|NAT|BNX|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051202e1c2001ev\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
