\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil Real Opens Stronger On Possible Interest Rate Cut  \b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2005\par 21:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2005, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Nov 23, 2005 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real opened stronger against the U.S. dollar Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued the momentum that it gained late in the previous session, with investors expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce additional interest rate cuts after markets close.  \par \par \uc2 The real opened at BRL2.232 per dollar, stronger from Tuesday's close of BRL2.247 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The markets are expected to be more upbeat in Wednesday's trading session, according to analysts.  \par \par \uc2 Market watchers expect the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut the benchmark Selic interest rate by 50 basis points to 18.5% per year. The bank cut the Selic rate at its meetings in September and October.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect markets to react positively to minutes from the U.S. Federal Reserves meeting Nov. 1, which were released late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 In the minutes, the Fed's rate-setting panel expressed concern about raising interest rates too high. The minutes could signal an end to the Fed's recent monetary tightening cycle, creating a more favorable climate for emerging market investments, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Rising interest rates in the U.S. typically drain investments from emerging markets such as \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 However, additional \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's central bankers could put pressure on the real, according to analysts.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make an exchange-linked debt offering Friday or Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The bank sold out similar "reverse swap" offerings in auctions last Friday and this past Tuesday, with demand strong for the paper.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the bank sold 10,500 contracts valued at $501.5 million, while last Friday's auction netted $404 million for 8,400 contracts.  \par \par \uc2 The bank began holding the swap auctions last week for the first time since March to allow investors to exchange dollar-indexed bonds for more popular paper linked to domestic interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Investors' fears that Finance Minister Antonio Palocci, the architect of the government's market friendly economic policies, would soon leave the government appeared to have faded, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Palocci, who denied published reports he had offered his resignation last week, soothed markets with testimony before a congressional committee on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 However, Palocci still faces a congressional inquiry into corruption allegations leveled against him by a former aide. His appearance before that panel is expected to take place by Dec. 10.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jeff Fick, Dow Jones Newswires; (55 11) 3145-1481; jeff.fick@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 11-23-05 0656ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020051123e1bn003v6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
