\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Despite Headwinds, Real Seen Staying Strong  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,013 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2005\par 23:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Laurence Norman \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--It's become a commonplace of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Alongside periodic panics about U.S. deficits and the regular ripples stirred by Europe's political problems, the market works up a sweat every few months over whether Brazil's real is due for a fall.  \par \par \uc2 The latest round of real-fretting has come this week. Continued political problems for President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva and dollar buying from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have a few analysts warning the real could be hitting some headwinds.  \par \par \uc2 That may be, but most market-watchers agree it would be foolhardy to predict the real will come far off its current levels as 2005 ends and the new year begins.  \par \par \uc2 After a big drop in 2003, the dollar tumbled 8.7% last year against the real and another 17.7% so far this year. The real's 2005 climb has been all the more remarkable since it comes when the dollar has gained sharply versus most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar ended last week at a 55-month low of BRL2.169 before lifting to BRL2.204 in local markets Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The real's modest drop of recent days came alongside the latest bout of worries about the future of Finance Minister Antonio Palocci and his market-friendly economic policies.  \par \par \uc2 Palocci has come under increasing scrutiny after a former aide alleged the minister's former municipal administration in the city of Ribeirao Preto ran a kickback \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at financing political campaigns for the governing Workers' Party. Over the weekend, there were reports that he may resign.  \par \par \uc2 Those worries sent Brazilian markets lower Monday and the real dropped 2% in a day. However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eked out modest gains Wednesday after Palocci, in testimony before a senate committee, denied any wrongdoing and staunchly defended the government's economic policies.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Slowdown Concerns \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, Monday's market jitters had some banks wondering about the real's next step. In a research note Tuesday, Nick Benenbroek, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Brown Brothers Harriman, said that "whether the real's fall ... develops into something more significant will depend on whether political jitters are reinforced by economic fundamentals."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bennenbroek noted a recent slowdown in industrial production growth, with September's 0.2% annual rise the lowest in more than two years. He also said Thursday's retail sales data could show a slowing from August's pace. Overall, with the domestic economy shouldering the burden of 19% nominal interest-rates, Bennenbroek said "recent data suggest that Q3 (third quarter) GDP growth will slow from its surprisingly strong Q2 (second quarter) pace."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday's price movements, Bennenbroek said, opened up the possibility of the dollar enjoying a "significant correction" and testing BRL2.3050 by year end.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, after months of standing on the sidelines, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regularly since early October, buying dollars via public auctions, though the bank has not said how much it has bought. Authorities say the dollar buying aims to build reserves to make future debt payments. However, some analysts believe the recent spurt of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is also aimed at stemming dollar gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Clyde Wardle, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at HSBC in New York, said all these factors mean investors may have become "a little over-extended" on the real in recent weeks. HSBC sees the dollar rising to BRL2.25 per dollar at year end.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Stay Calm: Real Has Tailwinds Too \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet none of these factors offers convincing reasons for investors to turn seriously bearish on the real as the market heads into a new year. Lawrence Goodman, Head of Global Emerging Markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at Bank of America in New York, said many fundamental factors remain real-friendly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With commodity prices lending a hand, Brazil has run a $38.2 billion trade surplus so far this year, with a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  survey Monday forecasting a record $42 billion surplus by year end. Meanwhile, with the local stock market hitting record highs, the economy set to grow over 3% in 2005 and interest-rates among the highest of any major economy, capital inflows - from foreign direct investment to hot money flows - remain copious. Brazil's current account surplus in the year-to-September equaled 1.78% of GDP.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some worry that, in 2006, the carry trade - borrowing in low yield \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and buying assets of high yielding ones like Brazil - may start turning against Brazil. That's because Brazil is likely to continue cutting rates while the Fed - and possibly the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the Bank of Japan - tighten their monetary stances. Just as the real has benefited from high levels of global liquidity and rising rates, it could suffer as the tide turns, they warn.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet not only are Brazil's nominal rates still very high, so are the country's real interest-rates - currently around 12%. Moreover, Goodman said, any reduction in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's carry trade advantage could be outweighed by the positive impact of falling interest-rates on Brazilian growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And while it's likely that global liquidity will gradually be squeezed next year, the world economy is still growing at a strong tick, meaning prices of the commodities that Brazil exports should remain firm.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goodman said the real may indeed face some choppiness in the days ahead but there remains "greater scope for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to appreciate." The bank sees the real setting a new high at BRL2.15 per dollar in coming weeks. Nor is HSBC's Wardle too bearish on the real. The bank sees the dollar staying at BRL2.25 at the end of the first quarter.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If that's right, prepare to ride out for a few more round of real-fretting in the months ahead. . (Laurence Norman covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. He has previously covered the Argentine economy for Dow Jones in Buenos Aires.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires, 1-201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com [ 11-16-05 1704ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1141  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020051116e1bg00031\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
