\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  extends rally, stocks drift lower  \b0\par\par\uc2 489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2005\par 15:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Brazil, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  edged firmer on Friday in its 10th straight session of gains, buoyed by an inflow of dollars, while stocks slipped as investors pocketed gains from a recent run-up.  \par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real <BRBY> gained 0.28 percent in thin turnover ahead of Monday's holiday in the United States to a fresh 4-1/2-year high of 2.163 to the dollar, leaving it over 22 percent stronger since the start of the year.  \par \par \uc2 "Not even politics gets to influence the market ... The (dollar) flow is very positive and nothing can beat the flows," said Tarcisio Rodrigues, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Paulista bank.  \par \par \uc2 He said there were some political concerns about the opposition's victory in Congress earlier in the day, when it managed to extend an investigation into alleged corruption in the government's Postal Service until April.  \par \par \uc2 Another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader in Sao Paulo added: "The market only has one direction -- everyone selling dollars."  \par \par \uc2 The real continued to benefit from a heavy inflow of dollars from exports and corporate bond sales overseas. It has also been propped up by foreign investors, who have been buying exposure to Brazil's lofty 19 percent benchmark interest rate rather than the low yields available in Europe and the United States.  \par \par \uc2 The real has rallied even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly to buy dollars on the spot market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to build up \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves and, indirectly, weaken the real, whose surge is irking exporters.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday said it would from now on make decisions on whether to hold "reverse exchange swap auctions" based on market conditions rather than on a weekly timetable.  \par \par \uc2 The swap auctions allow investors to enter contracts that rise or fall based on the rate of the U.S. dollar and domestic interest rates. The idea of the swaps is to reduce the country's exposure to a falling dollar.  \par \par \uc2 On the Sao Paulo Stock Exchange, the benchmark Bovespa <.BVSP> index fell 0.7 percent to 30,511 as investors took profits on the heels of a recent rally.  \par \par \uc2 Shares in state-run oil giant Petrobras weighed on the broader market, dropping almost 3 percent to 30.85 reais. The losses came after Petrobras reported a third-quarter net profit that came up short of the market consensus. Still, most analysts said the results were solid and could not justify the big drop, which some attributed to easing oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 Stocks in Companhia Vale do Rio Doce eased 0.3 percent to 82.2 reais after Canada's Canico Resource Corp. agreed to be taken over by CVRD which had sweetened its offer to as much as C$940 million ($790 million). (Additional reporting by Daniela Machado)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BRAZIL (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|U|D|M|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051111e1bb001n0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
