\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-NYBOT coffee rises to 3-month high on fund buying  \b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2005\par 16:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 8 (Reuters) - U.S. coffee futures rose more than 4.0 percent to a three-month high Tuesday, buoyed by fund buying, bullish technical signals and lingering worries about supply tightness, market sources said.  \par \par \uc2 The New York Board of Trade's active arabica contract for December delivery <KCZ5> settled up 4.90 cents at $1.0785 a lb., the loftiest level for the contract since Aug. 12, when it ended at $1.0955.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in the session, the contract peaked at $1.08.  \par \par \uc2 "We saw some spec buying, and we hit some fund buying when we got above $1.05," said Boyd Cruel, senior softs analyst at Alaron Trading.  \par \par \uc2 Stop-loss buy orders were elected once the contract broke above $1.0670, a double top set earlier in the month, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Among other arabica futures, March likewise climbed 4.90 cents to end at $1.1165 a lb. and more distant deliveries gained 4.80 to 5.45 cents.  \par \par \uc2 Front-month arabicas have risen 12.5 percent from last week's bottom trade of 96 cents a lb., fueled mainly by reports of crop losses and delays in new coffee harvests in Central America and southern Mexico following recent hurricanes.  \par \par \uc2 Traders and analysts expect crop damage in the Americas to total between 1 and 2 percent of an estimated 108 million 60-kg bags of 2005/06 world coffee output.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, producers in top coffee grower Brazil have been reluctant to sell because of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  disadvantage between the value of the dollar and the real <BRBY>.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil's real has rallied more than 20 percent since the start of the year, despite numerous \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to bolster foreign reserves and, indirectly, weaken the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "There is very little selling coming out of Brazil. We saw some today, but not anything notable," a trader said, pegging psychological resistance in NYBOT's December arabica at $1.10.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-COFFEE-NYBOT (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|C|SOF|GRO|MTL|PCM  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051108e1b8001ng\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
