\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Brazilian real seen weakening as rate cuts continue  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Toni Vorobyova  \par \uc2 756 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2005\par 13:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 28 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real is set to weaken in coming months as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to buy dollars, interest rates fall and markets start to price in a risk premium before elections in 2006, a Reuters poll shows.  \par \par \uc2 The median forecast in the Oct. 24-27 survey of 15 emerging market strategists showed the real <BRL=> <BRBY> softening to 2.31 per dollar in three months, 2.39 in six and 2.46 a year from now, compared with Friday's levels of around 2.28.  \par \par \uc2 "(There will be) a narrower interest rate differential as there is monetary easing in Brazil and monetary tightening in the U.S., a narrower current account surplus (and) some residual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Brazilian authorities," said Mario Mesquita at ABN AMRO in Sao Paulo.  \par \par \uc2 "Finally we are going to have elections here next year and expect some nervousness in the market derived from that."  \par \par \uc2 A long corruption scandal in Brazil has undermined President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva's standing for the run up to a presidential election in October 2006.  \par \par \uc2 Strategists said markets could start pricing in a risk premium related to the elections from the first quarter.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Oct. 3 after a six-month break. Strategists estimate it has bought at least $2.5 billion in October, pushing the real off a pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  4-year high of 2.213 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Nine of the strategists expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue regular \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in early 2006 or even for the whole of next year.  \par \par \uc2 However, opinion was split on where the real would be now if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not started buying up dollars, with estimates ranging from 1.85 per dollar to current levels.  \par \par \uc2 "(Without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) appreciation would have continued on the back of robust U.S. dollar inflows from the trade surplus and new debt issuance," said Suhas Ketkar at Royal Bank of Scotland in New York.  \par \par \uc2 But others said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was having less impact on the market than monetary policy and the real would still be at around current levels without it.  \par \par \uc2 MORE RATE CUTS  \par \par \uc2 Falling inflation enabled the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates for the first time in 17 months in September. This month it lowered the key Selic rate by more than expected, to 19 percent from 19.5.  \par \par \uc2 The poll showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cutting rates by 50 basis points in both November and December -- a view supported by the tone of the October minutes published on Thursday -- with the pace of easing then slowing in spring next year.  \par \par \uc2 The median showed rates at 18 percent at the end of 2005 and 16.75 percent at the end of March.  \par \par \uc2 By then, official U.S. interest rates are widely expected to rise to 4.5 percent from the current 3.75. This would reduce the rates differential between Brazil and the United States to 1225 basis points from 1525.  \par \par \uc2 "The cost of going long dollars in Brazil will be much lower than it is now," said John Welch at Lehman Brothers in New York.  \par \par \uc2 But whilst a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may boost exports and thus help the economy, it could also spark inflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark IPCA consumer price inflation is expected to come in broadly in line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 5.1 percent target for the end of this year, slowing from 7.6 percent in 2004, 9.3 in 2003 and 12.5 in 2002. For 2006, the inflation target is 4.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "It will require tight monetary policy to ensure they meet the CPI targets," said Alexandre Lintz at BNP Paribas in Sao Paulo. He sees the pace of rate cuts slowing to 50 basis points each quarter during 2006.  \par \par \uc2 The median showed a pause in rate cuts later next year, with the Selic seen at 16 percent at both end-June and end-September and at 15.5 percent at the end of 2006. End-2006 rates forecasts ranged from 14.50 to 16.50 percent.  \par \par \uc2 On the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  front, all 14 strategists who gave a view expected the real to be weaker than current levels in 12 months, with end-October 2006 forecasts ranging from 2.33 to 2.70 real per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-BRAZIL-POLL|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|ABX|BNX|FUN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051028e1as0019k\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (brazil near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or 